---
// Trust page for phux Team and Enterprise: what phux holds for an org, how it
// is protected, and the compliance status. Keep it honest: a control is
// listed only when it ships, and planned work says "planned". Every statement
// is derived from code: phux docs/audit.md and SECURITY.md, and the
// control plane schema in no-phux/phux-org. When a subprocessor is added,
// update this page 30 days before it starts processing, and bump LAST_UPDATED.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE, docsHref } from "../lib/site";
import "../styles/privacy.css";

const LAST_UPDATED = "2026-11-18";
const CONTACT = "privacy@phall.io";
const SECURITY = `${SITE.github}/security/policy`;
const title = `${SITE.name} — Trust`;
const description =
  `How ${SITE.name} protects an organization's data: what the control plane holds, the security controls, subprocessors, and compliance status.`;

const STATUS = [
  { item: "Signed, hash-chained audit log on every host", state: "Live" },
  { item: "Signed releases, signed channel manifests, SBOM", state: "Live" },
  { item: "LTS channel, security fixes for 12 months", state: "Live" },
  { item: "Data processing agreement", state: "On request for pilots" },
  { item: "Penetration test by a third party", state: "Planned" },
  { item: "SOC 2 Type I", state: "Planned" },
  { item: "SLA for the hosted control plane", state: "Enterprise, at launch" },
];

const SUBPROCESSORS = [
  { name: "Fly.io", what: "Hosting the control plane and the blind relay", where: "US" },
  { name: "Supabase", what: "Control plane database; push gateway", where: "US" },
  { name: "WorkOS", what: "SAML, SCIM and directory sync", where: "US" },
  { name: "Apple, Google", what: "Push notifications to approvers' phones", where: "Global" },
  { name: "Stripe", what: "Billing", where: "US" },
];
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description}>
  <article class="wrap policy">
    <header class="policy-head">
      <h1>Trust</h1>
      <p class="updated">
        Last updated <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{LAST_UPDATED}</time>
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        How phux for <a href="/teams">teams</a> protects your organization's
        data, and where our compliance work stands.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="summary" aria-labelledby="short">
      <h2 id="short">in short</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The organization control plane does not receive terminal streams,
          keystrokes, pasted text, clipboard text or file contents. Your hosts
          and clients handle that data.
        </li>
        <li>
          The control plane holds identity, policy and audit metadata: who
          ran which command, on which host, and what was decided.
        </li>
        <li>
          Local sessions can continue if the control plane is unavailable.
          Managed access uses cached policy and certificates only while those
          credentials remain valid; synchronization and enrollment need the
          control plane.
        </li>
        <li>
          phux for teams is in early access. SOC 2 and a third-party
          penetration test are planned, not done.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The hosted demo processes terminal content and has separate coverage
        in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>. Recordings and storage you
        configure are under your control, not the organization control plane's.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="data">
      <h2 id="data">what phux holds for an organization</h2>
      <p>
        Member emails and roles, group names and policies, device names and
        their public certificates, host names and software versions, and the
        audit records your hosts send.
      </p>
      <p>
        An audit record names the person and credential, the command, its
        target (such as <code>terminal:3</code> or a file path), the outcome
        and the time. The{" "}
        <a href={docsHref("/audit")}>audit log</a>{" "}lists what is never
        recorded. When a license sets a retention period, the control plane
        periodically prunes expired records; deletion is not immediate.
        SIEM delivery is being qualified. Do not rely on immediate or lossless
        delivery: host uploads, collector availability and retention affect
        what can arrive.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="controls">
      <h2 id="controls">security controls</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Encryption in transit.</strong> Managed device-to-host
          connections use mutual TLS. Control-plane enrollment and reporting
          APIs authenticate with tokens or signed requests; the self-hosted
          service needs HTTPS termination for production use. The relay
          forwards encrypted streams it cannot read.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Short-lived access.</strong> After company sign-in, a device
          gets a certificate valid for 12 hours, scoped by your group policy.
          Revocation takes effect when each host successfully synchronizes.
          Hosts poll every 15 seconds by default and can retain cached state
          during synchronization failures. Certificate expiry still applies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tamper-evident audit.</strong> Each host signs its records
          with Ed25519 and chains each to the one before. The control plane
          checks both on arrival and flags any break.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Approvals.</strong> Risky commands can wait for a person to
          approve them, from a desktop or a phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Secrets.</strong> Join, session and admin tokens are stored
          only as hashes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Supply chain.</strong> Releases are signed, the installer
          and <code>phux update</code> check signatures, and every release
          publishes an SBOM.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="status">
      <h2 id="status">compliance status</h2>
      <div class="table-region" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Compliance status">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {STATUS.map((s) => <tr><td>{s.item}</td><td>{s.state}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Pilots can ask for our security questionnaire answers and the draft
        DPA at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="subprocessors">
      <h2 id="subprocessors">subprocessors</h2>
      <p>
        These providers will process organization data once the hosted control
        plane launches. The planned control-plane services process organization
        metadata. Relay hosting carries end-to-end encrypted terminal streams,
        not their plaintext contents. Hosted-demo processing is covered
        separately above. We will update this list 30 days before adding a
        provider.
      </p>
      <div class="table-region" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Subprocessors">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Provider</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Location</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {SUBPROCESSORS.map((s) => <tr><td>{s.name}</td><td>{s.what}</td><td>{s.where}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Your own identity provider, SIEM and recording bucket are yours, not
        our subprocessors. The consumer apps' providers are in the
        <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="report">
      <h2 id="report">reporting a vulnerability</h2>
      <p>
        Follow the <a href={SECURITY} target="_blank" rel="noopener">security
        policy</a>. We acknowledge reports within 5 business days.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
